⏺ I read the repo's LICENSE files, the Twenty docs in packages/twenty-docs, and every file marked with the Enterprise tag. Most of the code is open source (AGPL v3) and you can use, change and rebrand
  it. 308 files are under Twenty's commercial license, and you can't run those in production without a paid Enterprise subscription. I'm not a lawyer, so have counsel check this before you ship a
  commercial product.

  1. How the code is split (LICENSE)

  The root LICENSE and packages/twenty-ui/LICENSE both say:

  ▎ This project is mostly licensed under the GNU Affero General Public License (AGPL) v3, except files that start with /* @license Enterprise */, which fall under "The Twenty.com Commercial License".

  2. Code you can use (AGPL v3)

  Everything without the Enterprise tag: the CRM core, objects and records, views, workflows, email and calendar sync, the API, and most of the UI.

  Allowed:
  - Use it commercially.
  - Change it.
  - Self-host it.
  - Rebrand it.
  - Sell it.

  Required:
  - You must publish your source (AGPL §13). If users reach your changed version over a network, which includes a hosted CRM, you must offer them the full source of that version, including your
    changes. A "Source" link in the app is the usual way.
  - Your changes stay AGPL. Your modifications and any code combined with them must also be released under AGPL v3. You can't make them closed-source.
  - Keep the notices. Leave the existing copyright and license notices in place and mark your files as modified.
  - Paying removes the publishing requirement. The docs list "No requirement to publish custom code as open-source" as a perk of the paid Organization (Self-Hosted) plan. That's the only way out of
    this requirement.

  Other licenses:
  - packages/twenty-companion is under the MIT license (copyright Recall.ai), which is permissive. You only need to keep its copyright notice.
  - Third-party libraries keep their own licenses.

